#!/usr/bin/env bash
# The factory's merge queue, for repositories where GitHub offers none, run once per call by launchd as the agents'
# GitHub account (it refuses the Human's):
#   bin/factory-queue sweep [--repo owner/name...] update the oldest auto-merge PR left behind main, and re-run a
#     [--once]                                     tier check after the Human approved its head (in the daemon, see below; one pass here
#                                                  with --repo or --once)
#   bin/factory-queue plist [--repo owner/name...] print the launchd job that runs that sweep every minute
#     [--daemon]                                   as a LaunchDaemon run by this account (#24), for the Human to load
#   bin/factory-queue check [--repo owner/name...] run one sweep in that job's environment, before the Human loads it
# With no --repo, which is how the job runs, a sweep covers every repository taken in (#211), so one with the kit
# needs no new install. The rule is the ledger's (clonesUnder in bin/lib/ledger.mjs): a folder of the factory's root
# ($FACTORY_ROOT, else this clone) that holds a .git and the kit's .gate/config.sh, named by its origin on github.com.
# A kit clone whose origin cannot be read or does not name owner/name is skipped with a line that names its folder.
# None found is a sweep of nothing that says so, not an error. --repo gives an explicit list in place of the search.
# A rollback is a revert pull request through the gate. Turning the ruleset off is the Human's emergency step alone,
# with auto-merge off on every open pull request first: with the ruleset off, every one of them would land unjudged.
# The job's command, `sweep` with no --repo, no longer runs the pass itself (#399): it asks the daemon (bin/factory-daemon, bin/lib/daemon.mjs) on
# $FACTORY_STATE/daemon.sock with `ping` and exits 0 when it answers; if nobody does, it starts bin/factory-daemon detached, in a session of its
# own, with the job's environment (the pass reads GitHub with it) and its output in ~/Library/Logs/factory/daemon.log (moved to daemon.log.1 over
# 5 MiB), waits up to 5 s for it to answer and exits 0, or 1 and queue-run.failed when it does not. A daemon that holds daemon.lock and does not
# answer is told the same way and nothing is started. The daemon runs the pass below every 60 s, under the same queue.lock, so a pass run by hand
# and the daemon's never overlap. `sweep --once` runs one pass in this process, and so does `sweep --repo ...` (a manual sweep); `check` runs one
# with --once in the job's environment and also says whether the daemon answers. The daemon exits after a pass that moved the checkout (below),
# and the job's next run starts the new code. Only the job starts the daemon: a bare `sweep` from anywhere else (XPC_SERVICE_NAME is not the
# job's label) starts nothing, says to use --once and exits 2.
# The lock: a sweep that holds it records its pid in queue.lock; the next sweep takes over a lock whose pid is not running,
# and one it cannot read once it is ten minutes old (takeLock in bin/lib/queue.mjs), so nobody removes it by hand.
# The job: `plist` prints io.github.tuannd9288.factory.queue, which lists no repository: every 60 s from this clone, with the
# gh and node the shell finds, logging its own lines to ~/Library/Logs/factory/queue.log (the pass's output is in daemon.log, #399). With
# --daemon it is a LaunchDaemon run by the
# account that printed it, factory, with that account's HOME, and never KeepAlive: a sweep exits after each run, so it would
# restart at once, in a loop. Before loading it, run `check` from the same shell: it runs one sweep in the job's environment,
# makes the log's folder, which launchd does not, and says whether the job is ready. factory's gh login keeps its token in a
# file (gh auth login --insecure-storage), since the keychain is locked over SSH and under launchd. The Human installs the
# plist with `sudo install -o root -g wheel -m 644 <plist> /Library/LaunchDaemons/io.github.tuannd9288.factory.queue.plist`,
# loads it with `sudo launchctl bootstrap system <that path>` and stops it with
# `sudo launchctl bootout system/io.github.tuannd9288.factory.queue`. That stops the job; the daemon it started runs in a session of its own and
# lives on a lease from the job's pings (#399): when none has come for 180 s (FACTORY_DAEMON_LEASE_MS) it stops, after the pass in hand, so a
# bootout ends it within about three minutes. To stop it at once, check that the pid recorded in $FACTORY_STATE/daemon.lock is the
# daemon (a stale lock after a crash can name a reused pid): `ps -p <pid> -o command=` shows `node <checkout>/bin/lib/daemon.mjs`, a command
# that ends in bin/lib/daemon.mjs, since bin/factory-daemon execs node. Then send it SIGTERM. Re-installing a changed plist goes in this
# order: bootout the job, check and SIGTERM that pid as above, install, bootstrap; otherwise the old daemon keeps the old plist's
# environment, the new job's pings keeping its lease alive.
# The checkout the daemons and bin/seat run from, ~/WORKSPACES, follows main (#257) in the first step of each sweep
# (bin/lib/checkout.mjs, in ~/WORKSPACES only, or where FACTORY_CHECKOUT names a clone, which is how the tests run it): git
# fetch, then git merge --ff-only --no-overwrite-ignore of the tip it fetched, and nothing else moves the checkout. It
# refuses, moving nothing and leaving queue-checkout.failed (Colly's status line and the digest show it): local changes, a branch
# that is not main, a failed fetch, a main that is not a fast-forward, a failed pull. It moves a file in the way (an ignored
# one that a commit brings in) to $FACTORY_STATE/archive/<sha7>-<time>/ and deletes nothing. It waits until liveRuns (every
# run-*.pid in ~/factory-runs/*/, and claims, of any seat) is 0, under $FACTORY_STATE/checkout.lock, which every start of a
# run (bin/seat) holds from before it registers until its claim is written, and no longer (the plugin install and the spawn
# come after); a refused start names the lock's holder; a start that loaded older code than the checkout now holds starts
# again once (FACTORY_REEXEC). A deferral says nothing until two hours, then queue-checkout.failed says how far behind and why.
# The Lead's Stop and SessionEnd hooks run a copy of transcript-copy.mjs in its run folder (hooks/transcript-copy-<sha7>.mjs,
# reused, never removed), since its pane stays open for days. The sweep also renders the two LaunchDaemon plists with the new
# code and compares them with the installed ones (/Library/LaunchDaemons, read only): while they differ, or a plist cannot be
# rendered (`could not render <label>`), queue-plist.failed says so and $FACTORY_STATE/plist-wanted/ holds what the Human
# installs with sudo, in the order above (the daemon stopped before the new job starts); it goes away on the first sweep after he does.
# Until then the old plist still starts the same files, so a change to the plist's arguments or environment must keep the old ones working.
# Then, where it follows main, the sweep removes the worktrees of landed work (#279, bin/lib/worktrees.mjs), in the checkout
# and each clone under it: a linked worktree whose branch is the head of a merged pull request (gh pr list), whose HEAD holds
# no commit that is on none of the clone's remote refs (git rev-list --count HEAD --not --remotes prints 0; no fetch), that is
# clean and that no live run holds. It runs git worktree remove, never --force; the branch and the run folder stay. Merged work
# left dirty or unpushed is kept and named in queue-worktrees.failed, whose first line names the first path.
exec node "$(dirname "$0")/lib/queue.mjs" "$@"
